From c32ebfa75bac95d297ce0874465e4da902deba60 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 15:06:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(rs): raise a fetched bad token as TypeScript does, and
 list a give-up once

A bad token a custom lex matcher returns (Lexer::bad, Lexer::bad_span) was
buffered in the lookahead like a good token. It failed every alternate it
met, so the error named the first token of the lookahead instead:
`unexpected` at the good token in front of it, where TypeScript's fetch in
parse_alts raises the bad token at once with its own code at its own
position. tabnas-css met this with an unclosed comment returned as the
second token of an alternate. Under recovery nothing recorded or stepped
past it, and the skip loop met the same token again until maxSkip gave up.

ensure_lookahead now handles a custom bad token exactly as it handles the
lexer's own faults, and both as TypeScript does:

- fail-fast, raised at the fetch with `why` (or `unexpected`) as the code,
  at the token, whichever alternate position asked for it;
- under recovery, recorded, coalesced with the run it continues, and
  stepped past with TypeScript's advanceLexPast rule (Lexer::skip_bad,
  replacing recover_after_error): past the token's span, and for a fault
  inside a string past the next row character after it, so the line after
  an unprintable newline is skipped whole;
- under relexing, left in the lookahead for an alternate to re-cut, and
  stepped past when recovery skips it;
- the trailing-content check raises it without recording it first, as
  Parser.start does (it used to absorb a lexer fault there);
- a lexer fault reaches the lex subscribers in every mode, as every token
  lex.next returns does.

A recovering parse that gave up could list its terminal error twice:
parse_recover_uncaught pushed it unless errors.last() == Some(&error), and
the recorded copy carried `recovered` metadata or had been dropped as a
cascade, so `a{b:c} d{` in tabnas-css listed `unexpected` 1:10 twice.
TypeScript records an error as it is constructed and gives up by
rethrowing the last one it recorded, so nothing is listed twice.
attempt_recover and absorb_lex_error now follow TypeScript's order (record,
drop a cascade, then read the maxRecoveries cap), a give-up marks the
parse, and only an error nothing recorded is listed where the parse ends.
The equality test is gone, so two distinct failures at one token (suppress
0) are still two errors.

Over 4,000 generated inputs in four modes (fail-fast, recovery, relexing,
both) on the shared json-builder grammar with a bad-token matcher,
TypeScript and Rust now report the same errors, positions and recovery
metadata on all 16,000 runs; 3,013 differed before.

test/spec/bad-token.tsv pins the behaviour in all three runtimes
(ts/test/bad-token.test.js, go/bad_token_spec_test.go,
rs/tests/bad_token_spec_test.rs); rs/tests/bad_token_fetch_test.rs adds
the API-level checks and the two cases Go answers differently (relexing
with recovery, a cascade at the recovery cap). DIVERGENCE.md records the
repair; test/AGENTS.md lists the new runners.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
